Line CX is an angle bisector in this triangle. This means that it splits $\angle ACB$ into two congruent angles. We can use this information to find the angle of $\angle XCB$ and then use that angle to find the length of $AX$. [asy]
import markers;

real t=1-.32;
pair A=(-4.07,0);
pair B=(40.008,0);
pair C=(0,20.616);
pair X=t*A+(1-t)*B;

draw(C--A--B--C--X);

label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,E);
label("$C$",C,N);
label("$X$",X,S);

//markangle(n=1,radius=15,A,C,X,marker(markinterval(stickframe(n=1),true)));
//markangle(n=1,radius=15,A,C,B,marker(markinterval(stickframe(n=1),true)));

label("$30$",.5*(A+C),SW);
label("$30$",.5*(A+C),SE);
label("$45$",.5*(B+C),NE);
[/asy]
<answer>Since CX bisects $\angle ACB$, we know that $\angle XCB = 22.5^\circ$. Now, we can use the law of sines to find the length of $AX$.
$\frac{AX}{\sin(120^\circ)} = \frac{CX}{\sin(22.5^\circ)}$
$AX = \frac{CX}{\sin(22.5^\circ)} \cdot \sin(120^\circ)$
$AX = \frac{12}{\sin(22.5^\circ)} \cdot \sin(120^\circ)$
$AX \approx \boxed{16.5}$[/answer]

